  "text": "Question eight. What are the sequel isolation levels? The standard names four: read uncommitted, read committed, repeatable read, and serializable. Each is defined by the anomalies it rules out, not by how it's built, and defaults differ: Postgres uses read committed. Question nine. What anomalies do isolation levels prevent? Three classic ones. A dirty read sees another transaction's change before it commits. Here another transaction set the balance to fifty, uncommitted. Postgres never shows it: at all four levels, even read uncommitted, the read says a hundred. A non-repeatable read: read a row twice, another transaction commits a change in between, and the second read differs. Read committed allows it; repeatable read prevents it. A phantom: rerun a query, and new rows match, because another transaction inserted them. The standard stops phantoms only at serializable; Postgres's repeatable read already does. Serializable promises an outcome some one-at-a-time order could give. In exchange, Postgres may abort a transaction with a serialization failure, to be retried.",
